---
name: ptt-pr-review
description: "As the integrator, list, show, or review local pull requests, and carry out the user's decision (merge, request changes, reject, withdraw). Use to list, review, integrate, or merge requests. Arguments: list [open|merged|closed|all], show N, N, all."
---

# Review local requests

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the records this skill
writes; other work needs AtE (rules file). `<records>/pr/repos` maps a
request's `repo` to its primary checkout `<repo>` and main branch `<main>`.
Request numbers may be short (`4`, `pr-4`, `000004`).

Listing and showing need no role. Reviewing and what follows need the
integrator role; your holder name (`integrator-N`) is `session:` in your
messages. Without it, run `<records>/bin/ptt who`: if the role is free, ask
the user to run `/ptt-take integrator` (better in a fresh session); if
another session holds it, say so. Then stop.

| Argument | Action |
| --- | --- |
| none, `list [open\|merged\|closed\|all]` | `<records>/bin/pr list ...`: report the table, with each open request's status and any cleanup line. |
| `show N` | `<records>/bin/pr show N`: report the conversation. |
| `N` | Review request N (below), then wait for the user's decision. |
| `all` | Review every request "waiting for review", lowest first; wait for a decision on each. |

A review is read-only for code: no merge, commit, checkout, or branch change
in `<repo>/` or any worktree. It writes only its own message and run records
(`<records>/bin/test-run new`). Never edit an existing message; never delete
branches, worktrees, or tags (that is `ptt-pr-cleanup`, after a decision).

## Review request N

At the start of an integration session, read `<records>/handoff/integrator.md`.

1. Read the whole conversation in order: `<records>/bin/pr show N`. Review
   only if `pr list` says "waiting for review"; otherwise report the status
   and stop.
2. Take `head` and `base` from the latest requester message giving each.
   If `git -C <repo> rev-parse <branch>` differs from `head`, the request is
   stale: say so in the review instead of reviewing a moving branch.
3. If `git -C <repo> merge-base --is-ancestor <main> <head>` fails,
   recommend bringing the branch up to date.
4. Review `<base>..<head>` (later rounds: the commits since the last
   reviewed head, and the answers to earlier comments): each diff, `git diff
   --check`, consistency with the project instructions and design documents,
   and the history (`git log --stat <base>..<head>`). Each commit is one
   logical change with its tests and documentation, and its message says
   what it changes. Messy history (fixup or WIP commits, a change and its
   revert or rework, mixed or split changes, merges of `<main>`, a commit
   that cannot build or pass on its own) gets a required comment asking for
   a rebase with fixup/autosquash, naming the commits and the intended
   result; that means recommending changes. Never ask to squash meaningful
   separate commits.
5. Re-run verification when the change or its risk warrants it: for each run
   `<records>/bin/test-run new <checkout at head>`, with commands,
   environment, logs, outputs, exit status, and passed/failed/skipped counts
   in it; build and run outside the reviewed checkout (e.g. in the run
   record); cite its id.
6. Write `MMMM-review.md` (one more than the highest `MMMM`; comment IDs
   continue across rounds). List `MMMM-*` again: if another file has your
   number and is already recorded, rename yours to the next free one. Record
   only this file: `<records>/bin/ptt record -m "pr NNNNNN: review MMMM"
   <file>`. Report the analysis, comments, and recommendation, end with the
   decision list, and stop:

   ```
   Decide request NNNNNN:
   1. Request changes (recommended): C1, C2
   2. Merge anyway
   3. Reject
   4. No decision, discuss further
   ```
7. On the user's decision (an option number with AtE, possibly with changed
   comments), write `MMMM-decision.md` and record it before anything else:
   `<records>/bin/ptt record -m "pr NNNNNN: decision <merge|changes
   requested|reject|withdraw>" <file>`. Then:
   - **Merge**: check the ancestry again (step 3; `<main>` may have moved);
     if it fails, record that the requester must update the branch, and
     stop. Otherwise, in `<repo>` on `<main>` with a clean worktree: `git
     merge --no-ff <head> -m "Merge <branch> (pr NNNNNN)"` (exactly this
     message: it is how the merge is found), then `<records>/bin/ptt pr-move
     NNNNNN merged -m "pr NNNNNN: merged as <merge SHA>"`.
   - **Request changes**: the request stays open; the author replies.
   - **Reject** or **withdraw**: `<records>/bin/ptt pr-move NNNNNN closed -m
     "pr NNNNNN: <rejected|withdrawn>"`.
8. Before the session ends, or when the user asks: open concerns and pending
   housekeeping go into `<records>/handoff/integrator.md`, recorded. When you
   name who acts next (the designer, a worker), run `<records>/bin/ptt who`
   first; what it said earlier in the session may be stale (G18). After a
   recorded decision, or when nothing waits, offer once to be cleared if
   nothing is uncommitted, your concerns are in `integrator.md`, and nothing
   was agreed only in conversation; state these three checks, and leave a
   handover (`<records>/bin/ptt handover <your name>`): a fresh session, or
   this one after `/clear`, then continues with `/ptt-resume` and no name.

## Templates

`MMMM-review.md`:

```markdown
---
request: NNNNNN
message: MMMM
kind: review
author: integrator
session: <holder name>
date: <YYYY-MM-DD>
head: <full SHA reviewed>
recommendation: <merge|changes-requested|reject>
test_runs: <run ids, or none>
---

# Review

## Analysis
What changed, risks, what was checked and what was not.

## Checks
Commands run and results; run ids.

## Comments
C1 (required): <comment>
C2 (optional): <comment>

## Recommendation
<merge|request changes|reject>, with reason. Waiting for the user's decision.
```

`MMMM-decision.md`:

```markdown
---
request: NNNNNN
message: MMMM
kind: decision
author: user
recorded_by: <holder name>
date: <YYYY-MM-DD>
head: <full SHA decided on>
decision: <merge|changes-requested|reject|withdraw>
---

# Decision

<The user's decision as stated, and any comments added, removed, or changed
relative to the review.>
```
